  "text_md": "2. Stochastic control problem\nN\nIn our framework, we start by considering the continuous trajectories of the interacting agents. We denote by xk(t) ∈ Rd the position of agent k at time t, with k = 1,...,N, and the time interval is deﬁned as [t0,T]. The dynamics of the agents are described by the following stochastic diﬀerential equation (SDE)\n![](<2503.09549_pg3_images/imageFile2.png>)\n1 N\nT\nwhere the term σdWk(t) represents the stochastic noise component, with Wk(t) being standard d-dimensional independent Brownian motions. The initial con-\n1 N\nΩ\n3\n![](<2503.09549_pg3_images/imageFile1.png>)\nN\ntion between agents is mediated by a nonlinear interaction kernel p(xk(t),xℓ(t)) which describes the forces governing their behavior. For further examples and theoretical background, we refer to [7]. The system operates within a welldeﬁned probabilistic framework. Let (Ω,F,P) be a complete probability space, where Ω represents the sample space, F is the σ-algebra of measurable events, and P is the probability measure. The stochastic processes Wk(t), are adapted to the ﬁltration F = (Ft)t≥t\nThe dynamics of the agents are thus driven by both deterministic controls uk(t) and the stochastic inﬂuences represented by the Brownian motions Wk(t). The control uk(t) is applied individually to each agent, with the aim of minimizing a cost functional over a ﬁnite time horizon. The control objective is to drive the system towards a desired state x¯ ∈ Rd while minimizing the associated cost. The latter (to be minimized with respect to the control term) incorporates a tracking term, which measures the deviation of each agent from the desired state x¯, and a regularization term to penalize the magnitude of the control. Speciﬁcally, the cost functional, which depends on x = (x1,...,xN), u = (u1,...,uN), is given by\nC(x,u) =\nt0",
